; `#` loads definitions without executing the module.
;
; Two calls, not one: `std-one-word` is an arrow — the working, then the
; contract prompt that reshapes it — and `std/shape` documents that cost. What
; this program tests is that importing a module fires NOTHING by itself, so the
; count is the macro's own and the import adds none.
; expect: calls 2
; expect: clean
; expect: answers
((# std/shape)
 (std-one-word "Name one primary colour."))
